---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Scopes from '@/components/motion/scenes/Scopes.astro';
import ScopesControls from '@/components/motion/scenes/ScopesControls.astro';
import { chipClass } from '@/components/motionDemo';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="scopes"
  title="Motion: per breakpoint"
  description="Motion per breakpoint with --udx-motion, and the scope functions revert and inspect that tear down a section or report it in development."
>
  <MotionStage scene="scopes" label="Live example: Per breakpoint" height="sm">
    <Fragment slot="controls"><ScopesControls size="page" /></Fragment>
    <Scopes size="page" />
    <Fragment slot="readout"><span class={`${chipClass} tracking-normal`} data-scopes-readout>Press Inspect this page to read the report of inspect().</span></Fragment>
  </MotionStage>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <p>
    A page can use a different motion at each breakpoint, and a section of a page can be taken down as a whole. Two things make this
    possible: <code>--udx-motion</code> may name a defined motion as well as <code>none</code>, and the functions of
    <code>@udixio/motion/scope</code> act on everything under one root.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--udx-motion</code> may name a defined motion of the same kind as the element's own. At each trigger (an entry, an exit,
    a replay, a loop that starts or resumes), the element plays the named motion in place of its own. Tailwind picks the motion by
    breakpoint:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`<div data-motion="rise" class="md:[--udx-motion:fade-on-view]"></div>`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Here the element rises on small screens and fades from <code>md</code> up, because <code>fade-on-view</code> is a reveal: a
    reveal names a reveal, a presence or an emphasis does not. Names of another kind, or names that are not defined, are ignored: the
    element plays its own motion, and a development build reports it once. <code>none</code> still turns the motion off. The
    binding's own triggers and its view options (<code>axis</code>, <code>repeat</code>) stay those of its definition.
  </p>

  <h2>Use it in the frameworks</h2>
  <p>
    The examples define the reveal that a card names, and keep each card's setting in its class. Each framework tears a section down
    with <code>revert</code>, and reads the report with <code>inspect</code>.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-scopes" live={false} />

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <MotionOptions
    rows={[
      { name: '--udx-motion', values: 'none · a defined motion of the same kind', default: 'the motion of the element', description: 'The motion the element plays in place of its own, at each trigger. none turns it off.' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>The cards rise on the default spatial spring, and the fade that replaces the rise plays on the same spring.</p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial']} />

  <h2>revert</h2>
  <p><code>revert(root = document.documentElement)</code> destroys every motion under <code>root</code>, the root included: presence, emphasis, layout, reveal, scrub, loop, values, gestures and sequences. Each element is left in its stylesheet state. A connection that covers <code>root</code> stops observing it, and a connection made afterwards binds it again. Use it to tear down a section of a page, or in a test.</p>
  <pre><code>{`import { revert } from '@udixio/motion/scope';

revert(document.getElementById('gallery')!); // the gallery keeps its markup, and no motion`}</code></pre>

  <h2>inspect</h2>
  <p><code>inspect(root = document.documentElement)</code> returns a plain report for development: the reduced-motion policy and its source, each binding with its element (a short CSS path), its kind, name, verb and state, and the defined motions. It changes nothing. Importing the entry also sets it as <code>udixio.motion.inspect</code> in development builds.</p>
  <pre><code>{`import { inspect } from '@udixio/motion/scope';

console.table(inspect().bindings);`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>Under reduced motion, a reveal shows its element at once, and so does each card of this page. With <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on the element or an ancestor, the end state applies without animating. See <a href="/motion/accessibility">Reduced motion</a>.</p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A name of another kind, or one that is not defined, is ignored: the element plays its own motion. A rise takes only a reveal: <code>fade</code>, a presence, is ignored on it.</li>
    <li>A connection that covers the root of <code>revert</code> stops observing it; a connection made afterwards binds it again.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
